edit(46,[('Ground → lower-left diode → motor left-to-right → upper-right diode → positive rail; v_left−v_right=−24 V, so the winding returns energy to the supply.','The initial inductive current continues left-to-right; the two forward-biased diodes clamp the terminals to ground and the positive rail respectively.'),('Ground → lower-right diode → motor right-to-left → upper-left diode → positive rail; v_left−v_right=+24 V.','That path requires reversal of the specified initial current rather than its continuous continuation.'),('Both upper diodes conduct in a zero-voltage loop entirely along the positive rail.','The upper-left diode is oriented from the left motor terminal toward the positive rail, so it cannot supply the required current into the left terminal.'),('Both lower diodes conduct in a zero-voltage loop entirely along ground.','The lower-right diode is oriented from ground to the right terminal, so it cannot accept the motor current leaving that terminal.')],stem='Use the lower bridge drawing with ideal diodes oriented as shown: each lower diode conducts from ground to its motor terminal, and each upper diode conducts from its motor terminal to the positive rail. Initially motor current is 2 A from left to right. All four transistors then turn off. With a 24 V supply able to absorb returned current, which immediate continuation path and terminal-voltage sense apply?',why='Inductor current cannot jump from 2 A to zero or reverse instantaneously. The lower-left diode feeds the left terminal from ground, and the upper-right diode carries current from the right terminal to the positive rail. Thus left is clamped to 0 V and right to 24 V, giving motor terminal voltage −24 V for positive left-to-right current. Its terminal power is −48 W at that instant, transferring stored energy toward the absorbing supply.',typ='diagram',difficulty='medium',steps=['Retain the initial left-to-right motor current direction.','Trace ground through the lower-left diode, motor and upper-right diode to the positive rail.','Ideal diode clamps give v_left=0 and v_right=24 V.','Terminal power is (−24 V)(2 A)=−48 W.'],forms=[formula('v_M=v_left−v_right; p_M=v_M i_M',{'v_M':'motor terminal voltage using left-minus-right convention','v_left,v_right':'terminal potentials relative to ground','i_M':'positive left-to-right motor current','p_M':'power entering the motor electrical terminals'},{'v_M,v_left,v_right':'V','i_M':'A','p_M':'W'},['Ideal diodes, all transistor switches off, positive initial motor current, supply can absorb current. Negative p_M denotes energy leaving the motor terminals.'])],assumptions=['Added ideal switching state, current, supply voltage and absorbing-source model. This is a circuit-analysis exercise, not a hardware switching instruction.','At the immediate transition, ignore diode drops, winding resistance and back EMF for the energy-source interpretation.'])
